                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This review is for the game itself and not a reflective look at the Remaster, I'll be going through it soon but I have played the SNES original and will base the review off that. \n\nRomancing SaGa 2 is many things, arguably it is one of the greatest JRPGs ever made as are most SaGa games but it provides so much to the table that was completely unseen and unheard of back in the 90s, non linear free-scenario giving you complete freedom to go wherever and do whatever, a generation system where you rule Avalon throughout the years spanning numerous emperors and generations and whose attributes and skills pass down onto the next heir. Being able to do quests at your own leisure, able to go back and tackle a challenge later on, Romancing SaGa 2 has everything that more experienced JRPG fans desire to see and this is all in a game on the Super Nintendo!\n\nWhile I said I would refrain from mentioning this port, I must applaud Square Enix greatly for giving us few western SaGa fans a way to play one of the most critically acclaimed SaGa games now in English! Many of us had to resort to Gamefaqs and a barely complete SNES translation to play this game back in the day, and now at long last we have a way to officially play the game. Thank you Square Enix, it's half arsed but I'll take anything honestly.\n\nBack to the game, SaGa games are notoriously difficult but never unfair, but Romancing SaGa 2 is a very unfair but still extremely fun game. it's an acquired taste to those who are not Japanese, but for those who are experimental and love a challenge this game will be right at home for you. Be sure to read the manual, many of its core mechanics aren't explained to you because again, this is an old-school JRPG and not the kind that will hold your hand and give you quest markers. Speak to NPCs, explore the land, do quests, glimmer techniques, and have fun.\n\nAs mentioned previously, this game is really different from its predecesor and the game to come after it. It was the first to introduce the sparking system, where your character can learn a new technique in battle. It introduces the generation system, and it also introduces 7 main antagonists who you fight throughout the course of the game. The actual objective of this game is to prepare yourself for these antagonists, dubbed the Seven Heroes.\n\nRS2 is light on story but is the most story heavy game in the franchise, you are the Emperor of Avalon named Leon. You have two children, one a brave warrior and the other one of a gentle heart, the latter of which is named Gerard and is the one who will take up after you. For that is all there is to this game's story, after that your emperors are background-less as well as your party, because both of them are expendable in this near thousand year long war against the Seven heroes. The antagonists I just mentioned have only a short backstory as well, their name is not a weird contradiction but a homage to how they themselves were once heroes who saved Humanity. But they were betrayed and sealed away, now back again with a vow for revenge, it is up to you to stop them!\n\nIf you're someone who wants a diversion from the JRPG mainstream and is a challenge seeker, I recommend this game heavily. But if you're a newbie to the SaGa series, I would put off from playing this for now as it does expect a lot out of you. Play Romancing SaGa 3 if you're new as it shares many of this games core systems.",
